var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Overview","page":"Overview","text":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight holds the local optimizers (LO) of the ACCV 2026 paper Robust Local Optimization Done Right: the strategies the paper proposes, the alternatives it studies, and the research variants behind its ablations. They implement VisualGeometryToolkitCore's local-optimization contract, so each one composes with any scorer in VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's RANSAC. The repository has two halves:\n\n \nthe package (src/, ext/) the strategies, the joint-(θ,z) lift core and its continuation schedules, the cross-validated operating points, the strategies' TOML registry, and PoseLib's truncated refiners (a package extension)\nthe research project (accv2026/) the paper's arm rosters, configs, drivers, renderers, the RansacLib baseline and the manuscript; see Reproducing the paper\n\nThe package depends on VisualGeometryToolkitCore (the contracts, the losses, the width axes) and VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation (the problems, the RANSAC loop, the core baselines nothing (no local optimization) and Irls{K}). PoseLib is a weak dependency.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Scoring-versus-refinement","page":"Overview","text":"RANSAC asks two different questions of a robust objective. Scoring ranks minimal-sample hypotheses and must be sharp: a single wrong inlier set loses the pair. Refinement moves the selected hypothesis toward the solution and needs a soft objective: gradient from correspondences that the seed currently rejects. The stack keeps the two roles behind separate contracts, so a scorer and a local optimizer are chosen independently. With the camera and the pixel correspondences cs of the Getting started scene:\n\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation, LocalOptimizationDoneRight\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore: WelschShape, StudentTUniformScoring, JeffreysPrior, FlatLabelPrior\n\n# the core default: MSAC at the class's threshold + TLS-IRLS following it\nresult = find_essential(camera, camera, cs)\n\n# the paper's recipe: Student-t + uniform selection (outlier half-width 250 px),\n# Welsch-HQ refinement\nresult = find_essential(camera, camera, cs;\n    scoring = StudentTUniformScoring(EssentialProblem, 250.0; scale_prior = JeffreysPrior(), label_prior = FlatLabelPrior()),\n    local_optimization_strategy = HQ{WelschShape}(; efficiency_constant = WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_ESSENTIAL))","title":"Scoring versus refinement"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Strategies","page":"Overview","text":"strategy path what it does\nHQ{K} :hq joint-(θ,z) half-quadratic lift on loss K; the paper's method (Welsch-HQ)\nWelschHQNewton, WelschHQEcm, BarronHQ, GmHqEps :hq research variants of the lift\nGnc{Irls{K}} :gnc graduated non-convexity over a core IRLS\nStudentTUniform :irls the Student-t + uniform mixture IRLS (EM), self-scaling\nStuTuBCD :bcd block-coordinate ascent of the scorer's own objective (the score-matched optimizer)\nStuTuHQTabulated, StuTuRegemm :hq, :gnc mixture lifts\nBarronAdaptive :irls Barron's adaptive loss under IRLS\nPoseLibLm, PoseLibLZ, PoseLibLmAdaptLossWidth :irls, :gnc PoseLib's truncated refiners (external refiners)\n\nThe lifts run on VisualGeometryToolkitCore's chart-rows contract, which VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation implements per problem, so one code path serves every liftable problem; see Lift core and schedules.","title":"Strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Widths","page":"Overview","text":"A width-carrying strategy (HQ, Gnc, WelschHQNewton, BarronHQ, GmHqEps, BarronAdaptive, and the core Irls) holds its width mode as a type parameter, resolved once per call by VisualGeometryToolkitCore's loss_at:\n\nefficiency_constant = m (EfficiencyConstant(m)): the loss width is m·ŝ, with ŝ the scale of the consensus set the scorer selected (the paper's partition anchoring). m is always the plain multiple of the scale, whatever the loss shape;\nloss_width = w (LossWidth(w)): an absolute width, in the caller's units;\nneither: the width follows the scorer. The loss's decision point is placed at the scorer's decision_boundary; a scorer without one is refused at entry.\n\nGive at most one of efficiency_constant and loss_width. The cross-validated constants of Operating points and TOML are efficiency constants, tuned per model class.\n\nwith_width(lo, w) returns the same strategy at another width mode. The self-scaling strategies (StudentTUniform, StuTuBCD, WelschHQEcm, the mixture lifts) carry no width; they start from the consensus scale.","title":"Widths"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#External-refiners","page":"Overview","text":"PoseLib's truncated refiners are ExternalRefit strategies: they refine a seed at an absolute loss width through VisualGeometryToolkitCore's external-refit contract (external_workspace, prepare_external!, refine_external!), outside the RANSAC loop. The PoseLib package extension implements those methods. The paper project's RansacLib arms implement the same contract; see Baselines.","title":"External refiners"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Operating-points-and-TOML","page":"Overview","text":"tuning_constant(problem, shape; features, dataset) returns the cross-validated efficiency constant of a loss on a model class, with its provenance (the accv2026 config that produced it); the WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_* constants are its Welsch-HQ entries.\n\nLODR_STRATEGY_TAGS registers the strategies' TOML tags. Merge it with the core's to read a strategy table of either package, such as a find_* pipeline's local_optimization_strategy = { type = \"hq\", loss = \"welsch\", … }:\n\nlo = optimization_strategy_from_toml(d; class = :essential,\n    tags = merge(VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.OPTIMIZATION_STRATEGY_TAGS, LocalOptimizationDoneRight.LODR_STRATEGY_TAGS))\n\nIn such a table, efficiency_constant = \"cross_validated\" resolves through tuning_constant.","title":"Operating points and TOML"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Evaluation","page":"Overview","text":"The paper's evaluation engine (pair geometry, the one refit layer, post-LO error matrices, the sample-budget engine, live RANSAC runs, the Roster of arms and its gate-enforced readers, the selection stream) is VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation, written against the core contracts alone. It evaluates any strategy here without per-type code: a native strategy through locally_optimize!, an external one through refine_external!.","title":"Evaluation"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Defaults-of-record","page":"Overview","text":"Every strategy default lives in configs/default.toml, read through one function, _config_default(table, key), which throws on a missing table or key; there is no second set of Julia literals. The file is parsed at precompile time, so an edit forces a recompile.\n\ntable holds\n[optimization_strategy.<tag>] each strategy's defaults (max_iter, damping, schedule), keyed by its LODR_STRATEGY_TAGS tag\n[optimization_strategy.poselib] cheirality_vote_points: points in the cheirality vote of an E decomposition\n[optimization_strategy.poselib_adapt] PoseLibLmAdaptLossWidth's total budget, rounds, min_support and operating efficiency constant\n[[tuning_constants]] the cross-validated operating points with their provenance\n\nA paper with its own protocol states its values explicitly; the ACCV 2026 protocol of record is accv2026/configs/protocol.toml.","title":"Defaults of record"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Frames","page":"Overview","text":"Frame in, frame out: public functions return results in the frame their inputs came in. The one declared exception is the benchmark pool, stored in NormalizedImageFrame; every API where pool data enters or leaves names that frame. Calibrated geometry is on unit bearings, except for the vendored RansacLib essential-matrix arm, a declared third-party pinhole exception (see Baselines).","title":"Frames"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/baselines/#Baselines","page":"Baselines","text":"The paper compares its local optimizers with two families of established refiners, both run as ExternalRefit strategies (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's external refit contract: external_workspace, refine_external!) through the same refit layer (VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation) and under the same LO budget as every other arm: PoseLib's truncated refiners (in the package) and RansacLib's classical LO-MSAC (in the paper project accv2026/, see below). They are evaluation-only: they refine a selected seed at an absolute loss width, never inside the native RANSAC loop.\n\narm strategy what runs\nTLS-IRLS PoseLibLm(max_iter) PoseLib Levenberg–Marquardt on the truncated Sampson loss (TLSShape)\nTLS-LZ PoseLibLZ(max_iter) PoseLib's Le–Zach relaxed IRLS on the same truncated loss\nTLS-IRLS-ŝ PoseLibLmAdaptLossWidth() TLS-IRLS with a partition-anchored width, re-estimated between rounds (E only)\nTLS-LO LebedaIterLO(opts, rng_seed) (accv2026) RansacLib's iterative least-squares ladder alone\nTLS-LO⁺ LebedaLOPlus(opts, rng_seed) (accv2026) RansacLib's full LO: least squares at the enlarged width, inner non-minimal resampling, per-sample ladder","title":"Baselines"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/baselines/#PoseLib-truncated-refiners","page":"Baselines","text":"PoseLibLm and PoseLibLZ refine relative pose (refine_relpose), fundamental matrices (refine_fundamental) and homographies (refine_homography) at the absolute loss width the refit hands refine_external! (a LossWidth). max_iter is their LM budget; the paper runs them at the evaluation's equal LO budget.","title":"PoseLib truncated refiners"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/baselines/#RansacLib-LO-(RansacLibLO)","page":"Baselines","text":"accv2026/lib/RansacLibLO wraps Torsten Sattler's RansacLib LO-MSAC (Lebeda, Matas and Chum, BMVC 2012), run unmodified as a seeded local optimization for E, F and H.\n\nVendored byte-identical. RansacLib is vendored at upstream commit b952083 with its LICENSE and NOTICE (BSD-3-Clause), and declared in VENDORED.toml as upstream code outside the stack's naming rules. Vendoring removes the network dependency from the reproduction path.\nThe only code added is what RansacLib's template API asks of a user — the E/F/H solver plug-ins and their Sampson residuals — and a subclass that exposes the protected LocalOptimization / LeastSquaresFit entries so the LO runs from a caller-supplied seed.\nConfiguration is RansacLib's public LORansacOptions subset (LebedaLOOptions). PRESET_REFERENCE is RansacLib's own defaults; the paper builds budget-parity options from its protocol of record (accv2026/configs/protocol.toml, [arms.lebeda]). Both the options and the rng_seed are required: they are protocol values of the caller.\nDeterministic. RansacLib's per-call mt19937(rng_seed) is its only source of randomness, so identical inputs give identical outputs. The wrapper also reports the number of linear solves per call, the budget-parity audit.\nPart of the paper project, not the package. RansacLibLO builds a C++ shim locally and is not registered, so the registered LocalOptimizationDoneRight does not depend on it. The paper project devs it by path ([sources] in accv2026/Project.toml), and the two arms (accv2026/src/lebeda_lo.jl) implement VisualGeometryToolkitCore's external refit contract (external_workspace, refine_external!), like any third-party strategy.\n\nBuilding it compiles a small C++ shim and needs git and a C++17 g++; Eigen comes from Eigen_jll (set EIGEN_INCLUDE to use another copy). From accv2026/:\n\nusing Pkg; Pkg.instantiate(); Pkg.build(\"RansacLibLO\")","title":"RansacLib LO (RansacLibLO)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/baselines/#Loss-width-and-frames","page":"Baselines","text":"The refit's loss width is the absolute, unsquared truncation width in the pool frame — the same currency the PoseLib arms consume. RansacLibLO takes the squared width in the frame of its input points, so the wrapper converts once:\n\nF and H — the pool frame is the model frame: pool-frame points, seed and width go through unconverted. On H the shim scores the codimension-2 Sampson error of the transfer constraint, whereas PoseLib's truncated H path truncates the symmetric transfer error: the same width, a different residual.\nE — RansacLib's essential solver projects onto the essential manifold, which is valid only for calibrated coordinates. The wrapper feeds it calibrated points on the z = 1 plane, converted once from the pool frame by the stored pool-frame calibration Kc, and converts the width with exactly PoseLib's camera-aware rule, ½(1/f₁ + 1/f₂), so TLS-IRLS, TLS-LZ and TLS-LO⁺ consume the same pool-frame width.\n\nThis E arm is a declared pinhole exception. Calibrated geometry in the stack is otherwise on unit bearings from the camera's back-projection, never the z = 1 plane; RansacLib is third-party code run unmodified, so its essential solver's plane-point convention is accepted here, pinhole-only, and declared where it lives.","title":"Loss width and frames"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/baselines/#Citing","page":"Baselines","text":"If you use the RansacLib arms, cite RansacLib and Lebeda et al.:\n\n@misc{Sattler2019Github,\n  title  = {{RansacLib - A Template-based *SAC Implementation}},\n  author = {Torsten Sattler and others},\n  URL    = {https://github.com/tsattler/RansacLib},\n  year   = {2019}\n}\n@inproceedings{Lebeda2012BMVC,\n  title     = {{Fixing the Locally Optimized RANSAC}},\n  author    = {Karel Lebeda and Jiri Matas and Ondrej Chum},\n  booktitle = {British Machine Vision Conference (BMVC)},\n  year      = {2012}\n}\n\nOne disclosed difference from Lebeda's original code: its iterated least squares used Sampson-gradient reweighted fits, whereas RansacLib's refits are plain least squares, used as-is.","title":"Citing"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reproducing/#Reproducing-the-paper","page":"Reproducing the paper","text":"The research project accv2026/ produces every figure and table of the paper. It is a DrWatson project that devs the package by path ([sources] in accv2026/Project.toml). The package, its tests and docs, and accv2026/ are one Pkg workspace: they resolve together, in one Manifest.toml at the repository root that is not committed. accv2026/REPRODUCE.md is the authoritative experiment → artifact index and records the data provenance; this page summarizes it.\n\nThe package and the research project split by one rule:\n\nBelongs in the package Belongs in accv2026/\nthe local optimizers a second paper reuses paper drivers, figures, arm rosters\nregistered LO baselines (PoseLib) the unregistered RansacLib baseline; anything that exists to make one comparison defensible","title":"Reproducing the paper"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reproducing/#Layout","page":"Reproducing the paper","text":"path contents\naccv2026/src/ the shared prelude: config.jl (entry), registry.jl (every path), arm_rosters.jl (THE arm table and the paper's Roster), protocol_folds.jl (the run-key fold recipe), cell_stages.jl (the shared stage builders), figure_style.jl, …\naccv2026/scripts/ one executable driver per experiment: fig*, supp_*, the pool producers, the table drivers (timing_table.jl, e2e_ransac.jl), the SupeRANSAC cell superansac_e2e.jl, polish.jl, heb_inlier_stats.jl\naccv2026/scripts/tools/ the CLIs the drivers launch: run_sequential_cv.jl (the tuning), run_polish.jl, the renderers, the pool builders, make_facet_grid.py\naccv2026/scripts/reproduce/ the launchers of VisualGeometryToolkitEval's runner on the stage graph of record, configs/reproduce.toml (reproduce_results_dgx.sh on Slurm, reproduce_results_desktop.sh locally; --dry-run, --only=…)\naccv2026/configs/ the configs of record: one TOML per cell, and the protocols folded into every run key\naccv2026/data/ two per-machine symlinks: exp_raw (immutable inputs) and exp_pro (add-only results), or VGTK_DATA_ROOT / VGTK_RESULTS_ROOT\naccv2026/lib/RansacLibLO/ the classical RansacLib LO baseline (vendored C++ shim, Pkg.build(\"RansacLibLO\"))\naccv2026/envs/ auxiliary environments (SupeRANSAC)\naccv2026/paper/ the manuscript; renders land in paper/figures/\naccv2026/test/ the project's tests (julia --project=. test/runtests.jl)\naccv2026/REPRODUCE.md the experiment → artifact index and the data provenance\n\nThe package itself is src/ (the strategies in src/strategies/, the joint-(θ,z) lift core and its continuation schedules in src/lift/, the cross-validated operating points in src/tuning_constants.jl, PoseLib's truncated refiners in src/poselib_arms.jl), ext/ (the PoseLib extension), configs/default.toml (the strategies' defaults of record and tuning constants), test/ and docs/.","title":"Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reproducing/#Drivers-and-stages","page":"Reproducing the paper","text":"Every driver is stage-idempotent: a stage whose output exists is skipped, --force re-runs, --stage=NAME runs one stage, and --plan prints the stage plan without running anything. From accv2026/:\n\njulia --project=. scripts/fig3_composed_miscal.jl --plan\n\njulia --project=. scripts/fig3_composed_miscal.jl --stage=cv\n\nEach driver declares its data inputs (require_input, with provenance), tunes only through the single cross-validation path, audits every tuning artifact before consuming it (audit_operating_points, VisualGeometryToolkitEval's Evaluation), and ends by rendering its panel into paper/figures/. The whole paper is one command of the runner, bash accv2026/scripts/reproduce/reproduce_results_dgx.sh (or _desktop.sh); its --dry-run lists every stage and every missing input first.","title":"Drivers and stages"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reproducing/#Config-driven-cells-and-keyed-output-directories","page":"Reproducing the paper","text":"A cell is a config of record (configs/<cell>.toml) plus the driver that runs it. Its effective config — the TOML with the protocols of record folded in by src/protocol_folds.jl — is hashed into its run key (VisualGeometryToolkitEval Experiments.run_key, the SHA-256 of the sorted TOML; the [render] section is excluded). Results are written to keyed directories such as phototourism/sift/E/Miscalibration/sequential-cv-<key>/ under the add-only results root, never rewritten. Consumers re-derive a producer's key from its config name, so keys are derived, never copied.\n\nprotocol of record governs\nconfigs/tuning.toml every [tuning.<name>] entry: windows, spacings, expand-on-boundary rules, objective, budget sampling, cross-validation\nconfigs/cv_protocol.toml tuning at ζ = 1 and the per-dataset cross-validation splits\nconfigs/miscal_protocol.toml the ζ evaluation ladder\nconfigs/sample_budget_protocol.toml per-dataset stream depth and checkpoint schedule\nconfigs/protocol.toml the experiment protocol: the equal LO budget, the Lebeda ladder, every per-arm deviation, and the result-changing values of each experiment","title":"Config-driven cells and keyed output directories"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reproducing/#The-sequential-cross-validation","page":"Reproducing the paper","text":"Every two-view cell is tuned by the sequential CV, scripts/tools/run_sequential_cv.jl: two one-dimensional cross-validations in sequence rather than one product grid.\n\nScorer — the selector's width a* on the selection-only error → scorer_cv.jld2.\nRefiners — each arm's width at that a*, read back from the scorer artifact → refiner_cv.jld2. A SelectionWidth arm (its refit width is the selection width a*) has nothing to tune here.\nEvaluation — the ζ miscalibration ladder on the test scenes at the tuned points → operating_points.jld2, the sidecar every downstream cell reads.\n\nEach stage expands its window when the argmin lands on its boundary; a surviving clip is recorded per arm and the readers refuse it.","title":"The sequential cross-validation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reproducing/#Figure-config-driver","page":"Reproducing the paper","text":"The paper's figures stack per-dataset panels into one grid. The figure configs configs/fig3_miscal_grid.toml and configs/fig4_budget_grid.toml name each row's panel and the driver stage that produces it; they compose and run no experiment.\n\npaper rows (dataset · model) producing driver (stage) cell config\nFig. 1, perturbed-seed basins PhotoTourism · E fig2_basin.jl (basin_cv, ladder_test, render) pt_e_basin.toml\nFig. 2, scale miscalibration — fig3_miscal_grid.jl (compose) PhotoTourism · E fig3_composed_miscal.jl (cv, oracle, render) pt_e_miscal.toml\n ETH3D · F supp_ETH_F_miscal.jl (render_main) accv2026_eth_f_miscal.toml\n HEB · H supp_H_miscal.jl (render_main) heb_h_miscal.toml\nFig. 3, hypothesis budget — fig4_budget_grid.jl (compose) PhotoTourism · E fig4_budget.jl (render) pt_e_budget.toml\n ETH3D · F supp_ETH_F_budget.jl (render) accv2026_eth_f_budget.toml\n HEB · H supp_H_budget.jl (render) heb_h_budget.toml\nFig. 4, least-squares polish PhotoTourism · E polish.jl (test, summary, render) polish_pt_e.toml\nTable 3, end-to-end RANSAC PhotoTourism · E e2e_ransac.jl (live, summary, table); SupeRANSAC's rows superansac_e2e.jl (inputs, cv, eval) pt_e_e2e_table.toml, pt_e_superansac.toml\n\nfig3_composed_miscal.jl --stage=cv writes THE deployed operating-points sidecar of PhotoTourism E that the downstream PhotoTourism cells read, so it runs first. The supplement's figures and tables (the scoring-only miscalibration, the partition-anchoring ablation, the polish) and Table 1's timing are indexed in accv2026/REPRODUCE.md.\n\nSupeRANSAC is an outside dependency: superansac_e2e.jl runs it in its own environment (accv2026/envs/superansac, PythonCall/CondaPkg with a source build of pysuperansac), detects whether it is installed and says how to install it. Without it, Table 3 is built from the stored SupeRANSAC results the cell's config names.","title":"Figure → config → driver"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reproducing/#Data","page":"Reproducing the paper","text":"Nothing large is committed. Two-view scoring pools are built by pool-producer cells (scripts/pt_e_pools.jl, scripts/heb_h_pools.jl, scripts/scoring_pools.jl --config=<name>) from the public datasets — IMC PhotoTourism, ETH3D, HEB — via scripts/tools/generate_scoring_pools.jl. The PhotoTourism E pool alone is about 2 TB. The data roots resolve through VGTK_DATA_ROOT / VGTK_RESULTS_ROOT or the data/exp_raw / data/exp_pro symlinks (accv2026/data/README.md). The cross-validation stages need a CUDA GPU. What the paper shows is tracked as the rendered .tex figures and tables under accv2026/paper/figures/; the result artifacts themselves are not yet published. With the produced artifacts synced, every --stage=render regenerates its committed panel on the CPU.\n\nRebuild the manuscript after rendering:\n\ncd accv2026/paper && make","title":"Data"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/reproducing/#Tests","page":"Reproducing the paper","text":"The package's tests run from the repository root; the research project has its own suite, which checks among other things that REPRODUCE.md lists every driver:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\n\ncd accv2026 && julia --project=. test/runtests.jl","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lift/#Lift-core-and-schedules","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"The joint-(θ,z) machinery behind HQ, Gnc and the research lifts. It runs on VisualGeometryToolkitCore's chart-rows contract (rows_provider, lift_init, lift_refresh!, lift_jacobian!, lift_retract, …), which VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation implements for its problems, so one code path serves every liftable problem.","title":"Lift core and schedules"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lift/#Lift-state-and-z-blocks","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"","title":"Lift state and z-blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lift/#Continuation-schedules","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"","title":"Continuation schedules"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.LiftState","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"LiftState{D}\nLiftState{D}(n)\n\nThe lift's own buffers on a D-dof chart for n points: the line process z and its back-substitution terms (dvec, gzv), and the reduced normal equations (Hbuf, gbuf). The stacked rows, their Jacobian, the per-point norms and the participation mask are the chart-rows provider's (VisualGeometryToolkitCore RowsWorkspace{D, C}). lift_state builds one per (task, size).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.LiftState"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.lift_state","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"lift_state(::Val{D}, n) -> LiftState{D}\n\nThe lift's state for n points under the one reuse policy (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's task_buffers).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.lift_state"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.LiftZBlock","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"LiftZBlock\n\nThe z-block of a joint-(θ,z) lift. JointGaussNewton (the default) reads the loss's closed forms through lift_zblock; WelschConvexNewton is the convexified-Newton z-block of the Welsch lift.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.LiftZBlock"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.JointGaussNewton","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"JointGaussNewton()\n\nThe Gauss–Newton z-block: per point the joint system is [w̄·JᵢᵀJᵢ, zᵢ·Jᵢᵀrᵢ; ·, ‖rᵢ‖² + hreg + μ] with (rinv2, hreg) = lift_zblock(loss, w̄) and z-gradient zᵢ(‖rᵢ‖² − rinv2). Any object answering lift_zblock and lift_window is a loss for it — a DeployedLoss or a TabulatedLift.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.JointGaussNewton"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WelschConvexNewton","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"WelschConvexNewton()\n\nThe convexified-Newton z-block of the Welsch lift (Zach & Bourmaud, ECCV 2018, Eq. 15). Per point, for Φᵢ = w‖rᵢ‖² + κ²(w), w = z², κ² the Welsch conjugate — Φ carries no ½, so every derivative is twice the Gauss–Newton block's:\n\n∂Φ/∂θ = 2w Jᵀr,  ∂Φ/∂z = 2z(‖r‖² − rinv2),\nθθ = 2w JᵀJ,  θz = 4z Jᵀr,  zz = 2(‖r‖² − rinv2) + 4w·κ²″(w),\n\nwith rinv2 from lift_zblock and the z-block convexified to max(zz, 8‖r‖²) + μ so the Schur-reduced θ-Hessian stays positive semidefinite. The curvature w·κ²″(w) is Welsch's own (it is c²), so the loss must be a deployed WelschShape (checked).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WelschConvexNewton"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.lift_window","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"lift_window(loss) -> (w̄lo, w̄hi)\n\nThe clamp window of the normalized line-process weight w̄ = z² the lift runs in: (1e-9, 1) for a closed-form loss, the table's own grid for a TabulatedLift.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.lift_window"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.TabulatedLift","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"TabulatedLift(M; γ = 1)\n\nBuffers for an M-node tabulated half-quadratic conjugate, filled by tabulate!. A loss for the lift's Gauss–Newton z-block (any codimension): it answers lift_zblock, lift_window, lift_wmax and lift_conjugate. γ scales the conjugate (the lifting regularizer's strength: γ > 1 makes the lift more reluctant to switch points off).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.TabulatedLift"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.tabulate!","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"tabulate!(tab::TabulatedLift, ω; warm = false) -> w_max\n\nFill tab for the weight function ω(x) -> (ω, dω/dx) (a Float64 pair) of the squared residual x, which must decrease strictly from w_max = ω(0). The grid spans w_max·[1e-9, 1 − 1e-9] log-spaced; each node's r²(w) is a Newton root of ω(x) = w started from the adjacent node's root, or — with warm = true, for a table re-built after a small change of ω — from the SAME node's previous root.\n\n\n\n\n\ntabulate!(tab::TabulatedLift, m::StudentTMixture; warm = false) -> w_max\n\nTabulate the half-quadratic conjugate κ² of the Student-t + uniform mixture's IRLS weight — which has no closed form — from the weight and its slope, VisualGeometryToolkitCore's posterior_weight and posterior_weight_derivative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.tabulate!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.cubic_hermite","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"cubic_hermite(f0, d0, f1, d1, u, h)\n\nThe cubic Hermite interpolant on one interval of width h at the fraction u ∈ [0, 1], from the node values f0, f1 and node slopes d0, d1 (slopes per unit of the abscissa, so they enter scaled by h). Generic over the number type, so it runs on device inside a kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.cubic_hermite"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.lift_raw_window","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"lift_raw_window(tab::TabulatedLift) -> (wlo, whi)\n\nThe table's weight grid ends on the RAW (unnormalized) weight scale — the clamp window of a caller that works in raw weights; lift_window is the same window normalized by lift_wmax.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.lift_raw_window"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.LoSchedule","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"LoSchedule\n\nA continuation schedule for graduated local optimization: owns the current continuation multiplier and the advance/terminate bookkeeping.\n\nThe contract (all four verbs; see the header for the consuming loop shape):\n\nschedule_init(sched, mu0, max_iter) -> state: mutable schedule state from the seed-derived start multiplier mu0 and the TOTAL inner-iteration budget max_iter (schedules that split the budget into rungs derive the rung length here — defined once, never re-inlined by engines).\nscale_mult(state) -> Float64: the current continuation multiplier (the GNC μ, or the loss-width multiplier).\nisterminal(state; open=true) -> Bool: whether the schedule has reached its terminal objective. A stalled inner step may end the engine ONLY when this is true.\nadvance!(state, stalled::Bool; open=true): per-iteration bookkeeping. A stalled step at a non-terminal position advances the schedule (rung ladders: early rung advance; time-indexed anneals: jump to terminal) — the stall-advance semantics, defined here once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.LoSchedule"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.schedule_init","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"schedule_init(sched::LoSchedule, mu0, max_iter) -> state\n\nThe mutable state of the schedule sched for one run: from the start multiplier mu0 (derived from the seed) and the total inner-iteration budget max_iter. A schedule that splits the budget into rungs derives the rung length here. See LoSchedule for the consuming loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.schedule_init"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.scale_mult","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"scale_mult(state) -> Float64\n\nThe current continuation multiplier of a schedule state: the GNC μ, or the loss-width multiplier (1 at the terminal objective of an anneal).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.scale_mult"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.advance!","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"advance!(state, stalled::Bool; open = true) -> nothing\n\nOne iteration's bookkeeping of a schedule state. A scheduled advance moves the multiplier on; a stalled inner step (stalled = true) at a non-terminal position also advances it (a rung ladder moves to its next rung early, a time-indexed anneal jumps to its terminal width). open is the engine's observation that the schedule may still make progress; a schedule that does not read it ignores it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.advance!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.isterminal","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"isterminal(state; open = true) -> Bool\n\nWhether the schedule state has reached its terminal objective. A stalled inner step may end the engine only when this holds; open as in advance!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.isterminal"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.NoSchedule","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"NoSchedule()\n\nThe trivial schedule of a static-objective engine: multiplier 1, always terminal (a stalled step ends the engine immediately).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.NoSchedule"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.MuLadder","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"MuLadder(factor; direction = :down, cap = Inf)\n\nThe GNC rung ladder, a schedule that moves μ by factor per rung.\n\nμ starts at a seed-derived mu0 and moves toward its terminal value — direction = :down anneals μ → 1 (GM / annealed-width Welsch surrogates; terminal at μ = 1), direction = :up anneals μ → cap (the TLS surrogate; terminal when the caller reports the middle band empty via open = false, or at μ ≥ cap).\n\nschedule_init splits the TOTAL budget into rungs exactly as the GncIrls engines always did: n_rungs from the μ0 → terminal ladder at factor (down: ⌈log(μ0)/log(factor)⌉ + 1 including the terminal μ = 1 phase; up: ⌈log(cap/μ0)/log(factor)⌉), rung_len = max(1, ⌊max_iter/n_rungs⌋).\n\nadvance! moves to the next rung when the rung budget is spent OR the inner step stalled (the stall-early advance — see the file header).\n\nMuLadder(factor; direction = :down, cap = Inf, converged_rung = false)\n\nCONVERGED-RUNG mode (converged_rung = true): no per-rung budget split — each rung runs until the inner step stalls and ONLY a stall advances the schedule (the Yang–Carlone-faithful setting; max_iter becomes a SAFETY ceiling enforced by the consuming loop, not a parity budget). Equivalent to an infinite rung_len; the budget contract is mode-aware: parity mode spends exactly the split, converged-rung mode is bounded only by the ceiling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.MuLadder"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.with_converged_rung","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"with_converged_rung(s::MuLadder, converged_rung::Bool) -> MuLadder\n\nThe same ladder (factor, direction, cap) in the other rung mode: true advances a rung only on a stall, false splits the budget across the rungs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.with_converged_rung"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.gnc_schedule","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"gnc_schedule(loss, factor) -> MuLadder\n\nThe rung ladder for a loss's graduation.\n\nA BandAnnealed shape anneals μ UP to the hard-surrogate cap (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's gnc_mu_cap() — the same cap gnc_mu0 guards to); the μ-scaled families anneal μ DOWN to 1, the target loss. Only the direction/cap mapping is per-family — rung arithmetic (nrungs, runglen) is MuLadder.\n\nDispatches on gnc_surrogate like the rest of the gnc_* family, so a shape with no graduation path is a MethodError rather than a tag that falls through to a default branch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.gnc_schedule"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lift/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.GeometricAnneal","page":"Lift core and schedules","text":"GeometricAnneal(octaves)\n\nA width multiplier that decays geometrically from 2^octaves to 1 over the iteration budget.\n\nThe time-indexed continuation of the scheduled-Welsch family: at iteration t of T the width multiplier is 2^{octaves·(1 − (t−1)/(T−1))} — 2^oct at t = 1 decaying geometrically to exactly 1 at t = T (T ≤ 1 ⇒ 1).\n\nStall semantics (the D1 fix, defined here once): a stalled inner step at t < T JUMPS the schedule to its terminal position (t → T) and the engine continues at the terminal width until a terminal stall or the iteration budget — a stall never ends the engine at a wide surrogate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.GeometricAnneal"},{"category":"section","location":"api/internals/#Internals","page":"Internals","text":"Documented internals: the module docstring, the defaults mechanism and the contract generics. They are not part of the supported API.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/internals/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.LocalOptimizationDoneRight","page":"Internals","text":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight\n\nThe local optimizers of the ACCV 2026 paper \"Robust Local Optimization Done Right\", on VisualGeometryToolkitCore's local-optimization contract and VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's problems:\n\nthe joint-(θ,z) half-quadratic loss lifts (HQ and its research variants WelschHQNewton, WelschHQEcm, BarronHQ, GmHqEps) and graduated non-convexity (Gnc), one code path over VisualGeometryToolkitCore's chart-rows contract (the lift core and its continuation schedules live here);\nthe Student-t + uniform IRLS (StudentTUniform), its score-matched block-coordinate ascent (StuTuBCD), the mixture lifts (StuTuHQTabulated, StuTuRegemm) and the Barron-adaptive IRLS (BarronAdaptive);\nPoseLib's truncated refiners (PoseLibLm, PoseLibLZ, PoseLibLmAdaptLossWidth) as external refiners (their solver calls load with PoseLib, a package extension);\nthe cross-validated operating points (tuning_constant) and the strategies' TOML registry (LODR_STRATEGY_TAGS), merged with the core's to load: optimization_strategy_from_toml(d; class, tags = merge(VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.OPTIMIZATION_STRATEGY_TAGS, LODR_STRATEGY_TAGS)).\n\nCompose one with any scorer: find_essential(cam1, cam2, pairs; local_optimization_strategy = HQ{WelschShape}(efficiency_constant = WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_ESSENTIAL)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.LocalOptimizationDoneRight"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight._config_default","page":"Internals","text":"_config_default(table, key)\n\nThe default key of [table] (a dotted path, \"optimization_strategy.poselib_adapt\") in configs/default.toml. Throws a KeyError naming a missing table or key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight._config_default"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/internals/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.DEFAULTS_PATH","page":"Internals","text":"The defaults of record (configs/default.toml).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.DEFAULTS_PATH"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/internals/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.CONTRACT_GENERICS","page":"Internals","text":"CONTRACT_GENERICS\n\nThe generic functions of LocalOptimizationDoneRight another module may add methods to (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's contract_generic_violations): the PoseLib truncated refiners' pose body, implemented by the PoseLib extension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.CONTRACT_GENERICS"},{"category":"section","location":"api/strategies/#Local-optimization-strategies","page":"Strategies","text":"Every strategy implements VisualGeometryToolkitCore's local-optimization contract (lo_workspace, locally_optimize!), so it composes with any scorer in VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's ransac and find_* pipelines. The core baselines, nothing (no local optimization) and Irls{K}, live in VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation. A width-carrying strategy takes efficiency_constant = m (a multiple of the consensus set's scale), loss_width = w (an absolute width), or neither, in which case its loss width follows the scorer's decision_boundary. See the Overview.","title":"Local optimization strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"api/strategies/#Half-quadratic-lifts-and-graduated-non-convexity","page":"Strategies","text":"","title":"Half-quadratic lifts and graduated non-convexity"},{"category":"section","location":"api/strategies/#Student-t-uniform-and-Barron-adaptive-strategies","page":"Strategies","text":"","title":"Student-t + uniform and Barron-adaptive strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"api/strategies/#PoseLib-truncated-refiners","page":"Strategies","text":"External refiners (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's ExternalRefit): they refine a seed at an absolute loss width through refine_external!, outside the RANSAC loop. The solver calls load with PoseLib (a package extension). See Baselines.","title":"PoseLib truncated refiners"},{"category":"section","location":"api/strategies/#Cross-validated-operating-points","page":"Strategies","text":"","title":"Cross-validated operating points"},{"category":"section","location":"api/strategies/#TOML-registry","page":"Strategies","text":"","title":"TOML registry"},{"category":"section","location":"api/strategies/#Index","page":"Strategies","text":"","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.HQ","page":"Strategies","text":"HQ{K<:AbstractLossShape} <: AbstractOptimizationStrategy\n\nJoint half-quadratic local optimization of the model and per-point weights under the robust loss K.\n\nHalf-quadratic lifted local optimization on loss K (Zach 2014): the genuine joint-(θ,z) Gauss-Newton on the lifted residual (zᵢ·rᵢ, κ(zᵢ²)) with the loss's closed-form Geman–Reynolds regularizer (lift_zblock, VGC's loss family). Per-point weight zᵢ is a free variable, initialized LARGE (all-inlier = smoothed cost) and evolved jointly with θ, Schur-eliminating Δz — this broadens the basin, escaping a poor seed where the static Irls/StudentTUniform is trapped, without annealing or quadrature. ONE code path for every loss AND every problem (ManifoldLiftHQ on VisualGeometryToolkitCore's chart-rows contract, lift/lifts.jl); K contributes only its closed-form z-block quantities and tracked cost. Implemented for every liftable problem (the MinimalSolveProblem family: its lift provider supplies the chart).\n\nThe loss width is frozen at setup by the width mode (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's loss_at): at the consensus set's Student-t scale ŝ the efficiency_constant is the PLAIN multiple for every loss (as for Irls{K}), so the loss width is efficiency_constant·ŝ and sq_loss_width is efficiency_constant²·ŝ², with the multiple and the noise variance stored apart and never multiplied into a stored width:\n\nK = WelschShape: loss_width = efficiency_constant·ŝ (1.5 was the pre-anchoring point)\nK = SmoothTLSShape: hard cutoff loss_width = efficiency_constant·ŝ (historically 2.0 = the statistically natural 2σ cutoff; no width follows the scorer's decision point). Default max_iter = 40 (historical ψ̂ arm).\nK ∈ {GemanMcclureShape, CauchyShape}: loss_width = efficiency_constant·ŝ too. The half-weight currency these losses were once read in is retired (robust contract, 2026-11-11): a value stated as a half-weight depth m_½ is converted once, efficiency_constant = decision_width(K(), m_½) (the roster's GM arms compose it into their grid map; the identity for Cauchy).\n\nFields\n\nmax_iter::Int: max joint-GN iterations (25; 40 for SmoothTLSShape)\nμ::Float64=1e-9: Gauss-Newton damping\ninit::Symbol=:flat: z₀ policy — :flat (all-in, default) | :posterior (soft seed weights) | :consensus (hard r½ mask)\nwidth::W: the width mode (efficiency_constant = m, the plain multiple, loss_width = w, or neither: follow the scorer), as Irls\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.HQ"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WelschHQNewton","page":"Strategies","text":"WelschHQNewton(; efficiency_constant, max_iter=25, μ=1e-9)\n\nWelsch half-quadratic lift refined with the convexified Newton joint-(θ,z) step (Zach & Bourmaud, ECCV 2018, Eq. 15) instead of the Gauss-Newton step of HQ{WelschShape}. Identical fixed-scale setup (loss_width = efficiency_constant·ŝ from the seed partition); the only difference is the second-order z-block with the convexification h_z = max(2r² + 4·loss_width²·(1+ln z), 8r²). Intended as the A/B partner of the GN lift — it should match on easy seeds and (per the paper) reach better minima on hard, barrier-crossing seeds. EssentialProblem only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WelschHQNewton"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WelschHQEcm","page":"Strategies","text":"WelschHQEcm <: AbstractOptimizationStrategy\n\nAblation sibling of HQ{WelschShape}: the same closed-form Welsch joint-(θ,z) lift, but the loss width efficiency_constant·√ŝ² is re-estimated each iteration by the same Student-t + uniform ECM the mixture uses (_mix_ecm), instead of fixed at the seed. Closed-form κ² ⇒ no LUT, so the only added cost is the O(n) ECM sweep. Kept a separate LO strategy so fixed-vs-ECM scale can be ablated head-to-head.\n\nFields\n\nmax_iter::Int=25: max joint-GN iterations\nefficiency_constant::Float64: the loss width as a multiple of ŝ (efficiency_constant·√ŝ², ŝ² re-estimated per iter)\nμ::Float64=1e-9: Gauss-Newton damping\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WelschHQEcm"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.BarronHQ","page":"Strategies","text":"BarronHQ(α; efficiency_constant, max_iter=25, μ=1e-9)\n\nThe joint half-quadratic lift of HQ on Barron's loss at a fixed shape α.\n\nThe α-DIAL lift (smooth-and-lift 3-ii): the same genuine joint-(θ,z) half-quadratic lift as HQ with the Barron family loss at fixed shape α, standardized at a FIXED CORE WIDTH — the facet-B Barron loss width is efficiencyconstant·ŝ (a value stated in the retired m/√2 currency is migrated once, ×1/√2), which holds the near-zero (Gaussian-core) curvature IDENTICAL across every α, so the dial isolates tail steepness (retirement profile) only. Anchor identities (mandatory gates, liftlossestests.jl): α → −∞ runs the SAME iteration as `HQ{WelschShape}(; efficiencyconstant = √2·efficiency_constant)`; α = −2 matches the GM lift and α = 0 the Cauchy lift at their convention-mapped scales. Runs on the generic ManifoldLiftHQ driver, so on every liftable problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.BarronHQ"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.GmHqEps","page":"Strategies","text":"GmHqEps(; efficiency_constant | loss_width, max_iter = 40, μ = 1e-9)\n\nThe Geman–McClure joint-(θ,z) half-quadratic lift, parameterized by its loss width: a research arm beside HQ{GemanMcclureShape}, which standardizes the same loss at its half-weight radius instead.\n\nThe loss (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's GemanMcclureShape) is a soft-tailed redescender with a closed-form lift and a constant z-block Hessian; its tail decays polynomially and never reaches zero weight. At loss width w the half-weight radius is √(√2 − 1)·w ≈ 0.6436·w.\n\nThe loss width is efficiency_constant·ŝ (ŝ the consensus set's scale) or an absolute loss_width in the residuals' units; give at most one. With neither, the loss follows the scorer: its half-weight radius sits at the scorer's decision_boundary (a loss width of about 1.554 times it). max_iter bounds the joint Gauss–Newton iterations and μ is their damping; the defaults are configs/default.toml's [optimization_strategy.gm_hq_eps]. It runs on the generic lift driver, so on every liftable problem, and like every local optimizer it can end in a local minimum of its seed's basin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.GmHqEps"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.Gnc","page":"Strategies","text":"Gnc{S<:AbstractOptimizationStrategy} <: AbstractOptimizationStrategy\n\nGraduated non-convexity over the objective of the static strategy S.\n\nGraduated-non-convexity WRAPPER: canonical Yang–Carlone GNC (RA-L 2020) with the Black–Rangarajan surrogate weights, graduating the objective of the wrapped static strategy S — the paper's three GNC baselines for the continuation family:\n\nGnc{Irls{WelschShape}} — GNC-W (annealed-width Welsch)\nGnc{Irls{TLSShape}}    — GNC-TLS (Black–Rangarajan band surrogate of the HARD truncation; TLSShape is the hard-TLS facet tag of VGC's loss family)\nGnc{Irls{GemanMcclureShape}} — GNC-GM (μ-convexified GM)\n\nThe type parameter ADMITS any strategy (Gnc{HQ{K}} — graduation OVER the lift, a composite :gnc ∘ :hq mechanism — and the Resample{S} wrapper are NOT implemented here, the LO dispatch covers Gnc{<:Irls} only). The GNC arms run through the same locally_optimize! contract as every IRLS/lift strategy — one code path, one width resolution.\n\nWidth (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's loss_at): under EfficiencyConstant(m) the terminal (target) loss width is c̄ = m·ŝ with ŝ the consensus set's Student-t scale (exactly the width the lifted loss arms freeze); under LossWidth(w) it is w; with neither it follows the scorer's decision_boundary. There is no plug-in scale bootstrap.\n\nVariants and μ-schedules:\n\n:gm (GNC-GM) — surrogate ρμ(r) = μc̄²r²/(μc̄² + r²), weights `w = (μc̄²/(r² + μc̄²))² ≡ liftweight(GM, r², μ·c̄²)`; μ₀ = 2·r²_max/c̄² (convex start from the SEED residual extreme), μ ← μ/factor per rung, clamp at μ = 1 (true GM) and spend the remaining budget there.\n:welsch (GNC-W) — annealed-WIDTH Welsch, c²μ = μ·c̄², weights w = exp(−r²/(μc̄²)); μ₀ = r²max/c̄² (every seed point starts ≥ e^{−1}) or a numeric entry = m ⇒ μ₀ = max(m², 1) (entry-breadth dose axis), μ ← μ/factor, clamp at μ = 1 (target Welsch at c̄).\n:tls (GNC-TLS) — piecewise Black–Rangarajan band weights (gnc_tls_weight); μ₀ = c̄²/(2r²max − c̄²) with the r²max ≤ c̄²/2 guard (⇒ skip graduation, start effectively-hard), μ ← factor·μ, stop graduating once the middle band is empty or μ ≥ 1e6, then finish at the terminal surrogate.\n\nBudget parity: TOTAL model updates across ALL rungs = max_iter (the shared 25-update LO cap; every GNC stage counts). Rung arithmetic is VGC MuLadder (budget = :converged ⇒ converged-rung mode, max_iter a safety ceiling). Rung advance is scheduled OR stall-early; termination is TERMINAL-only (the 373d0a6 semantics via the LoSchedule trait). Best-tracking is on the TARGET loss (true TLS cost / true GM ρ / fixed-width Welsch cost at c̄) — graduation shapes exploration only. Implemented for every liftable problem (the MinimalSolveProblem family), like HQ{K}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.Gnc"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.StudentTUniform","page":"Strategies","text":"StudentTUniform <: AbstractOptimizationStrategy\n\nThe paper's Stu-tU-IRLS: posterior-weighted IRLS under the Student-t + uniform mixture (the same model family as the profile-marginal score). Computes posterior inlier probabilities from the marginal score partition, uses them as soft weights for a WLS refit, re-scores, and iterates over a fixed Student-t + uniform likelihood. Self-scaling: it re-estimates its own scale each iteration (contrast with the consensus-anchored Irls{K}/HQ{K}/ Gnc{S} strategies, whose loss width is frozen at efficiency_constant·ŝ from the consensus set).\n\nIt needs the scorer's uniform outlier model (outlier_halfwidth).\n\nFields\n\nmax_iter::Int=5: Max IRLS iterations per hypothesis\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.StudentTUniform"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.StuTuBCD","page":"Strategies","text":"StuTuBCD <: AbstractOptimizationStrategy\n\nExact same-objective refinement for RANSACScoringDoneRight's StudentTUniformScoring: block-coordinate ascent on the scoring objective S itself. Alternates\n\n(model)      θ⁺ = argminθ Σ{i∈I} rᵢ(θ)²        — hard-inlier least squares   (partition)  I⁺ = argmax_I  S(θ⁺, I)             — the score's own sort-and-sweep\n\nto convergence. Every step is non-decreasing in S: the model step lowers RSS_I with all other score terms fixed (so S rises), and the partition step is the score's exact argmax over partitions. Unlike StudentTUniform (soft, score-derived responsibilities under a related t/uniform mixture — NOT the Jeffreys-collapsed hard-partition objective) and the Welsch-HQ lift (a different objective optimized jointly over auxiliary weights), this is the strict same-objective' baseline: exact score monotonicity, hard partition. Expected to be very monotone but narrow-basined — correspondences excluded by the current partition exert no force on the pose (RSS_I` sees only I).\n\nFields\n\nmax_iter::Int=25: max block-coordinate sweeps per hypothesis\ntol::Float64=0.0: minimum score increase to keep iterating\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.StuTuBCD"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.StuTuHQTabulated","page":"Strategies","text":"StuTuHQTabulated <: AbstractOptimizationStrategy\n\nHalf-quadratic lifted LO using the exact Student-t + uniform mixture loss ρ(r)=−log(π_in t_ν(r;ŝ²)+π_out(2a)^{−d}). Same genuine joint-(θ,z) weight-lift as HQ{WelschShape}, but the regularizer κ² — whose closed form is transcendental for the mixture — is tabulated per ECM iteration: the monotone weight ω=u·w_resp is inverted on a grid (r²(w)=−κ²′(w)) and cumulatively integrated (κ²(w)). Scale ŝ² and mixing π_in are ECM/EM-updated each iteration. EssentialProblem only.\n\nThe lifting-regularizer strength γ scales the down-weighting penalty κ² (a GNC-style continuation knob): γ>1 makes the lift more reluctant to switch points off (softer z-block Hessian, hreg ∝ 1/γ), keeping it in a smoother, wider-basin regime for better escape from a poor seed. Selection is always on the true γ=1 objective (best-tracking), so γ shapes only the exploration — hence a constant, validated γ (rather than a schedule). κ² is built on a fine grid so γ is the sole, explicit regularizer knob. On PT val the LO is insensitive to γ over [1,5] (median 4.92–5.04°, within the basin-flip noise); γ=2.5 is the robust plateau choice (near the per-scene minima, non-edge).\n\nFields\n\nmax_iter::Int=25: max joint-GN iterations\nγ::Float64=2.5: lifting-regularizer strength (PT-val plateau; LO is γ-insensitive over [1,5])\nμ::Float64=1e-9: Gauss-Newton damping\nlut_points::Int=32: κ² grid resolution. The table is read by cubic Hermite interpolation using the exact node slopes κ²′=−r² (the stationarity condition), so 32 nodes match a ~1000-node linear table in accuracy at ~⅛ the per-iteration rebuild cost. γ remains the sole regularizer knob — grid coarseness is no longer an accuracy lever (the inflation trap of a coarse linear table is avoided).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.StuTuHQTabulated"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.StuTuRegemm","page":"Strategies","text":"StuTuRegemm <: AbstractOptimizationStrategy\n\nHalf-quadratic LO for the same Student-t + uniform mixture loss as StuTuHQTabulated, but solved by relaxed generalized majorization-minimization (ReGeMM; Zach & Le, ECCV 2020, §6.1) instead of the joint weight-lift. Faithful to the paper's robust-estimation scheme: weights are initialized all-inlier (u≡1) and inferred inexactly by graduated non-convexity. Each round (i) ECM-updates ŝ²,π_in and rebuilds the κ² table, (ii) sets uᵢ = ω(rᵢ/σ) — the touching weight at an inflated scale σ≥1, with σ the largest value (by bisection) still meeting the diminishing-gap criterion J(θ,uⁿᵉʷ) ≤ η·J(θ) + (1−η)·J(θ,uᵒˡᵈ), J(θ)=minₚ J(θ,u), then (iii) minimizes the bound over the pose for the graduated weights (the weighted-Sampson refit, Alg. 1's line-3 argmin), accepted only if it descends. σ anneals to 1 as the gap tightens, so this is weight-space GNC from the smooth init.\n\nThe monotone, convergent counterpart of the StuTuHQTabulated joint step. Zach & Le report it favorable vs the joint HQ lift on bundle adjustment (truncated quadratic); on 2-view essential the joint lift reaches a better optimum with a lighter tail. EssentialProblem only.\n\nFields\n\nmax_iter::Int=100: number of ReGeMM rounds (one σ-graduation + one pose refit each)\nη::Float64=0.5: diminishing-gap fraction (Zach & Le use η=1/2; η→1 ⇒ touching/standard MM = IRLS)\nlut_points::Int=128: κ² lookup-table resolution\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.StuTuRegemm"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.BarronAdaptive","page":"Strategies","text":"BarronAdaptive(; efficiency_constant | loss_width, max_iter=25, α_min=-10.0,\n               truncation_multiple=10.0, n_sub=256)\n\nIRLS on Barron's loss whose shape is re-estimated from the residuals each iteration.\n\nSolver-matched ADAPTIVE-SHAPE robust IRLS: the identical PosteriorIrlsMethod/block_coordinate_descent! machinery as StudentTUniform / Irls{WelschShape}, with Barron's generalized loss ρ(r; α, losswidth) (CVPR 2019) whose SHAPE α is re-estimated each iteration from the current residuals by minimizing the truncated-partition negative log-likelihood of Chebrolu et al. (RA-L 2021, Eq. 11-15): 1-D grid search over α ∈ [αmin, 2], with Z̃(α) = ∫_{-T}^{T} e^{-ρ(x, α, 1)} dx, T = truncation_multiple (the normaliser's domain, in units of the loss width). The loss width is efficiency_constant·ŝ, frozen at the seed partition's Student-t scale — Chebrolu et al. fix the width for exactly the scale/shape confounding our partition anchor resolves. n_sub caps the residual subsample used by the α grid search (strided; the full set is used when n ≤ n_sub).\n\nThis is the \"adaptive-static\" cell of the loss taxonomy: the loss adapts its shape within the solve (no lift, no graduation) — the strongest representative of \"why not adapt instead of lift?\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.BarronAdaptive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.BarronAdaptiveLikelihood","page":"Strategies","text":"BarronAdaptiveLikelihood <: AbstractWeightPolicy\n\nLikelihood adapter for BarronAdaptive. update_nuisance! is the α-step (Chebrolu Alg. 1 step 1): grid-argmin over α of log Z̃(α) + mean ρ(rᵢ/c; α) on the (strided) residual subsample. update_weights! sets the Barron IRLS weights at the current (α, c). Only the SHAPE α moves here; the WIDTH is frozen at construction from the arm's (efficiency_constant, ŝ²) pair (sq_loss_width) and is stored as its reciprocal because the unit-scale α math divides by it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.BarronAdaptiveLikelihood"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.PoseLibLm","page":"Strategies","text":"PoseLibLm(max_iter) <: AbstractOptimizationStrategy\n\nPoseLib Levenberg–Marquardt over the TRUNCATED Sampson loss (TLSShape) — the LO-RANSAC standard truncated refiner (TLS-IRLS). An external refiner (ExternalRefit): it refines at an absolute loss width (refine_external!), not in the native RANSAC loop. max_iter is its LM budget.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.PoseLibLm"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.PoseLibLZ","page":"Strategies","text":"PoseLibLZ(max_iter) <: AbstractOptimizationStrategy\n\nPoseLib's Le–Zach relaxed IRLS (TR-IRLS) on the same truncated loss as PoseLibLm — the optimizer axis within the truncated class (TLS-LZ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.PoseLibLZ"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.PoseLibLmAdaptLossWidth","page":"Strategies","text":"PoseLibLmAdaptLossWidth(; max_iter, rounds, efficiency_constant)\n\nPoseLib's truncated LM refiner with its loss width re-estimated between rounds.\n\nConsensus-anchored truncated refit (TLS-IRLS-ŝ): PoseLib LM over the truncated loss, its loss width re-formed between rounds LM rounds as efficiency_constant·ŝ of the current model's within-width residual scale (outer pose-block / scale-moment alternation; PoseLib is a black box).\n\nThe INITIAL width is anchored on the seed's consensus scale — ŝ² of the consensus set under the refit scoring at the outlier half-width of the delivered loss width (loss_width_to_outlier_halfwidth at the refit protocol's operating efficiency constant, [optimization_strategy.poselib_adapt] operating_efficiency_constant), VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's consensus_s2! — never a plug-in scale; a consensus too thin to carry a scale makes no refit. max_iter is the total LM budget across rounds. Defaults: configs/default.toml [optimization_strategy.poselib_adapt]. E-track only (PoseLib refine_relpose).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.PoseLibLmAdaptLossWidth"},{"category":"function","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.truncated_refit_pose","page":"Strategies","text":"truncated_refit_pose(lo, ws, problem, seed_E, loss_width) -> refined pose\n\nThe relative pose a PoseLib truncated refiner reaches from an essential-matrix seed.\n\nThe E-branch optimizer body of a PoseLib truncated refiner WITHOUT a guard or scoring bracket: the seed's cheirality-resolved pose (fast strided vote on the problem's bearings) refined by PoseLib refine_relpose at the absolute pool-frame loss_width, on the refiner's external_workspace. For callers that need the REFINED pose itself (a warm-started polish). Implemented by the PoseLib extension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.truncated_refit_pose"},{"category":"function","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.tuning_constant","page":"Strategies","text":"tuning_constant(problem::Symbol, shape::AbstractString;\n                features::Symbol = :any, dataset::Symbol = :any) -> TuningConstant\n\nThe cross-validated efficiency constant of a model class and loss shape.\n\nResolve the cross-validated efficiency_constant for problem (:essential | :fundmat | :homography | :radial_homography | :radial_camera | :absolute_pose) and loss shape (the tag vocabulary a strategy's loss key uses) along the provenance hierarchy (features, dataset) → (features, :any) → (:any, :any).\n\nshape does NOT wildcard. An efficiency constant is an operating point for one shape, not a unit that converts between them, so a class with no entry for the requested shape raises rather than serving a constant validated for a different one. Check .validated to see whether the returned value was cross-validated at its own key or transferred (.transfer names the source key), and .tuning for the product tuning that produced it.\n\njulia> using LocalOptimizationDoneRight\n\njulia> c = tuning_constant(:essential, \"welsch\");\n\njulia> c.efficiency_constant, c.validated   # the class tier holds a transferred value\n(1.597, false)\n\njulia> c.efficiency_constant == WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_ESSENTIAL\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.tuning_constant"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.TuningConstant","page":"Strategies","text":"TuningConstant\n\nA cross-validated efficiency_constant with codified provenance — the loss width as a multiple of the estimated scale; see VisualGeometryToolkitCore.deploy.\n\nFields\n\nefficiency_constant::Float64 — the loss width as a multiple of ŝ (partition-anchored).\nshape::String — the loss shape this value was validated for, in the tag vocabulary a strategy's loss key uses (\"welsch\", \"geman_mcclure\", …).\ntuning::TuningProvenance — the product tuning that produced the value (for a transfer: the one that produced the value it borrows).\ntransfer — nothing for a value validated at its own key, else the registry key (problem, features, dataset) it was transferred from.\nvalidated::Bool — transfer === nothing (derived, not stated).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.TuningConstant"},{"category":"type","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.TuningProvenance","page":"Strategies","text":"TuningProvenance\n\nWhere a cross-validated efficiency_constant came from: arm arm of the product tuning [tuning.<name>] of the cell config of record config (a path relative to the directory holding the stack checkouts), whose spec hashes to hash (VisualGeometryToolkitEval tuning_hash). pending_hash is nothing, or the hash the config of record's spec has since moved to: the value is pending re-validation under it (and hash still names the spec that produced it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.TuningProvenance"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_ESSENTIAL","page":"Strategies","text":"WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_ESSENTIAL\n\nThe Welsch-HQ efficiency_constant of the essential class.\n\nFor VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.find_essential, it is the deployed optimum of the PT-E-SIFT-4K product tuning (the essential class tier transfers it from the (:sift, :phototourism) entry, the one that tuned it). Equal to tuning_constant(:essential, \"welsch\").efficiency_constant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_ESSENTIAL"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_FUNDMAT","page":"Strategies","text":"WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_FUNDMAT\n\nThe Welsch-HQ efficiency_constant of the fundmat class (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.find_fundmat). TRANSFERRED from the essential entry — tuning_constant(:fundmat, \"welsch\").validated is false until the PT-F cross-validation lands.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_FUNDMAT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_HOMOGRAPHY","page":"Strategies","text":"WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_HOMOGRAPHY\n\nThe Welsch-HQ efficiency_constant of the homography class (VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.find_homography). TRANSFERRED from the essential entry — tuning_constant(:homography, \"welsch\").validated is false until the HEB-H re-CV succeeds at its gate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_HOMOGRAPHY"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_ABSOLUTE_POSE","page":"Strategies","text":"WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_ABSOLUTE_POSE\n\nThe Welsch-HQ efficiency_constant of the absolutepose class (`VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.findabsolutepose). TRANSFERRED from the essential entry — no PnP entry has been cross-validated yet, sotuningconstant(:absolute_pose, \"welsch\").validatedisfalse`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_ABSOLUTE_POSE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/strategies/#LocalOptimizationDoneRight.LODR_STRATEGY_TAGS","page":"Strategies","text":"LODR_STRATEGY_TAGS\n\nThe TOML tags of this package's local-optimization strategies.\n\nThis package's local-optimization strategy registry (VisualGeometryToolkitCore's TagRegistry, built by VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation's @lo_registry): TOML tag → strategy type or loss-parametric family, the tag snake_case of the type's name. Retired tags name their successor. Merge it with the core's to load either: merge(VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation.OPTIMIZATION_STRATEGY_TAGS, LODR_STRATEGY_TAGS).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LocalOptimizationDoneRight.LODR_STRATEGY_TAGS"},{"category":"section","location":"","page":"Home","text":"<div id=\"landing\" class=\"landing\">\n<header class=\"landing-hero\">\n  <div class=\"landing-hero__text\">\n    <p class=\"landing-name\" style=\"--landing-name-chars: 36\">Robust Local Optimization Done Right</p>\n    <h1 class=\"landing-title\">Score Sharp, Refine Soft</h1>\n    <p class=\"landing-tagline\">James Pritts · Kevin Köser\nMarine Data Science\nKiel University\nACCV 2026</p>\n    <div class=\"landing-actions\">\n<a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--brand\" href=\"#Getting-started\">Getting started</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"api/strategies/\">API Reference</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.05743\">Paper</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/LocalOptimizationDoneRight.jl\">Code</a>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"landing-hero__image\">\n    <div class=\"landing-hero__image-container\">\n      <img src=\"assets/landing/hero_manifold.png\" alt=\"A curved model surface with noisy points around it; points near the surface are drawn strong in orange by their robust weight, far outliers faint in grey.\" width=\"320\" height=\"320\">\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</header>\n<section class=\"landing-features\" aria-label=\"Features\">\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"overview/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🧮</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Compose a local optimizer</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">The paper's strategies (<code>HQ</code>, <code>Gnc</code>, <code>StudentTUniform</code>, <code>StuTuBCD</code>, ...) implement the core local-optimization contract and plug into any <code>find_*</code> pipeline beside any scorer: <code>local_optimization_strategy = HQ{WelschShape}(...)</code>.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"guide/reproducing/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">📊</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Reproduce the paper</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\"><code>accv2026/</code> holds one config-driven driver per figure and table: sequential cross-validation, keyed output directories, and renders straight into the manuscript.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"guide/baselines/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">⚖️</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Compare against classical LO</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">PoseLib's truncated refiners (<code>PoseLibLm</code>, <code>PoseLibLZ</code>) and, in the paper project, RansacLib's LO-MSAC (<code>LebedaLOPlus</code>, <code>LebedaIterLO</code>, vendored unmodified) run as baseline arms under the same budget.</p>\n  </a>\n</section>\n</div>\n\nRANSAC scoring and local optimization impose different robustness requirements. We isolate robust-loss shape, inlier-scale misspecification and optimization strategy for essential, fundamental and homography estimation.\n\n ζ 1/16 1 4 16 128\nStu-tU+Welsch-HQ (ours) error [°] 2.71 1.58 1.84 2.55 6.19\n mAA@10° 0.594 0.686 0.666 0.619 0.505\n time [ms] 934 160 114 74 47\nSupeRANSAC error [°] 53.73 2.23 3.49 10.14 38.27\n mAA@10° 0.210 0.643 0.558 0.333 0.072\n time [ms] 92 118 176 263 365\n\nRANSAC under scale miscalibration (paper, Table 3). Scene-averaged median pose error, mAA@10° and median wall time per pair on the 11 PhotoTourism test scenes, single-threaded; both systems tuned at ζ = 1, SupeRANSAC at its validated maximum noise level with at most 10 000 trials. ζ scales the inlier scale given to each system, from 16 times too small to 128 times too large. Bold marks the better system per column.","title":"Home"},{"category":"section","location":"#Abstract","page":"Home","text":"RANSAC scoring and local optimization (LO) impose different robustness requirements, motivating the separation of hypothesis selection from refinement. We systematically isolate the effects of robust-loss shape, incorrectly specified inlier scales, and optimization strategy on essential matrix, fundamental matrix, and homography estimation. A profile-marginal score marginalizes the nuisance inlier scale and selects an inlier partition, from which we estimate the scale that sets the LO loss width; this makes LO robust to an inlier scale specified too large, whereas one specified too small degrades selection itself. Refinement needs gradient from correspondences the seed currently rejects: optimizers that reweight from current residuals stay pinned to their seed, whereas methods with broad basins recover strongly perturbed seeds yet degrade accurate score-selected hypotheses, so basin size alone is insufficient to assess RANSAC LO. Joint half-quadratic optimization balances the two and is the most consistent strategy across model classes. An optimizer matched to the profile-marginal score, which never decreases it, does not reach the best accuracy, challenging the prescription that scoring and refinement objectives should match. Composed from these findings, our RANSAC reduces the median essential-matrix pose error of a state-of-the-art RANSAC on PhotoTourism from 2.23° to 1.58° with a correctly specified inlier scale and from 38° to 6.2° when it is grossly misspecified (128× too large).\n\nKeywords: robust estimation · RANSAC · local optimization · half-quadratic lifting · graduated non-convexity","title":"Abstract"},{"category":"section","location":"#Install","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Install"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-package","page":"Home","text":"The package requires Julia 1.13 or later. The Getting started example below also loads the packages it composes with:\n\npkg> add LocalOptimizationDoneRight VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation VisualGeometryToolkitCore","title":"The package"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-paper's-experiments","page":"Home","text":"Clone the repository and instantiate the research project accv2026/, which devs the package by path; the repository is one Pkg workspace, resolved in one Manifest.toml at its root that is not committed. Its classical RansacLib baseline (accv2026/lib/RansacLibLO, unregistered) builds a small C++ shim, which needs git and a C++17 g++ (Eigen comes from Eigen_jll):\n\ngit clone https://github.com/VisualGeometryToolkit/LocalOptimizationDoneRight.jl.git\ncd LocalOptimizationDoneRight.jl/accv2026\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate(); Pkg.build(\"RansacLibLO\")'","title":"The paper's experiments"},{"category":"section","location":"#Getting-started","page":"Home","text":"The paper's recipe, Student-t + uniform selection and Welsch half-quadratic refinement at its cross-validated efficiency constant, on a synthetic two-view scene:\n\nusing Random: MersenneTwister\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore: CameraModel, Point2, WelschShape, estimate, inlier_mask, motion, px\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore: StudentTUniformScoring, JeffreysPrior, FlatLabelPrior\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitCore.Synthetic: pose_error, sample_twoview_scene\nusing VisualGeometryToolkitEstimation: EssentialProblem, find_essential\nusing LocalOptimizationDoneRight: HQ, WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_ESSENTIAL\n\n# Two 500 × 500 px views of one pinhole camera (focal length 600 px, principal\n# point at the image centre): 200 matches with 1 px noise, then 100 mismatches\n# drawn anywhere in the image.\nrng = MersenneTwister(1)\nR, t, _, _, matches = sample_twoview_scene(rng, 200; f = 600.0, c = 250.0)\nnoisy(u) = u + Point2(randn(rng), randn(rng))\nanywhere() = Point2(500 * rand(rng), 500 * rand(rng))\ncs = [[u₁ => noisy(u₂) for (u₁, u₂) in matches]; [anywhere() => anywhere() for _ in 1:100]]\ncamera = CameraModel(600.0px, Point2(250.0px, 250.0px))\n\n# Student-t + uniform selection, its outlier half-width 250 px (half the image),\n# the scale integrated out against Jeffreys' prior and the labels flat, then\n# Welsch half-quadratic refinement at its cross-validated multiple of the\n# consensus set's scale.\nresult = find_essential(camera, camera, cs;\n    scoring = StudentTUniformScoring(EssentialProblem, 250.0; scale_prior = JeffreysPrior(), label_prior = FlatLabelPrior()),\n    local_optimization_strategy = HQ{WelschShape}(; efficiency_constant = WELSCH_HQ_EFFICIENCY_CONSTANT_ESSENTIAL),\n    rng = MersenneTwister(2))\n\nn_inliers = count(inlier_mask(result))   # the 200 matches, and the few mismatches that land on their epipolar lines\n# Rotation and translation-direction errors in degrees, against the closest of\n# the four motions (R, t) the estimated essential matrix factors into.\nrotation_error, translation_error =\n    rad2deg.(minimum(pose_error(Rᵢ, tᵢ, R, t) for (Rᵢ, tᵢ) in motion(estimate(result))))\n\nThe evaluation engine the paper runs on (pair geometry, the refit layer, the sample-budget engine, rosters) is VisualGeometryToolkitEval.Evaluation.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","text":"Start here: the example above.\nHow to: Reproducing the paper maps every figure and table to its config and driver; Baselines runs the classical LO arms (PoseLib, RansacLib) under the same budget.\nExplanation: the Overview introduces the strategies, their width modes and how they compose with a scorer.\nReference: Strategies, Lift core and schedules and Internals.","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Method","page":"Home","text":"Hypothesis selection and refinement are separate roles with separate robustness requirements, and the paper evaluates them separately.\n\nScore sharp. A profile-marginal score marginalizes the nuisance inlier scale and selects an inlier partition; the scale estimated from that partition sets the LO loss width (partition anchoring), so no externally calibrated threshold is needed.\nRefine soft. Local objectives and strategies — TLS, Stu-tU, Welsch and Geman–McClure losses under IRLS, joint half-quadratic (HQ) lifting, graduated non-convexity (GNC) and classical LO⁺ — are each composed with the same scorer, tuned by the same cross-validation, and run under the same 25-model-update budget.\nPolish hard. A final least-squares refit on the selected inliers is evaluated as a separate step.\nEvaluate in isolation. Perturbed-seed basins, scale miscalibration over ζ ∈ [1/128, 128], accuracy against the hypothesis budget, and end-to-end RANSAC against SupeRANSAC.","title":"Method"},{"category":"section","location":"#Results","page":"Home","text":"Local optimization needs gradient from tentative correspondences, those a minimal-sample model classifies as outliers. Methods that reweight from current residuals, including the objective-matched Stu-tU-BCD, stay pinned to their seed.\nJoint HQ lifting balances travel and precision. It is the most consistent strategy across model classes and performs best on PhotoTourism, where some score-selected seeds, especially at small budgets, lie outside the basin of static reweighting. GNC recovers the most distant seeds but is a liability near the solution, where RANSAC usually supplies its seeds.\nMiscalibration splits along the same line. Partition anchoring removes the reliance on an externally calibrated threshold and keeps LO robust to an inflated scale, whereas a deflated scale degrades selection, which no local optimizer repairs.\nSelection bounds what LO can gain, and polishing by a least-squares refit on the selected inliers does not measurably move the solution out of LO's basin.\nComposed RANSAC. The profile-marginal scorer with partition-anchored, half-quadratically lifted LO substantially outperforms a strong baseline on essential-matrix estimation.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"#Related-projects","page":"Home","text":"Part of the Visual Geometry Toolkit, Julia packages for robust visual geometry from Kiel University:\n\nBlobBoards: robust markers for accurate pose (BMVC 2026)\nRANSAC Scoring Done Right: marginalize the scale, then choose the inliers (NeurIPS 2026)","title":"Related projects"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citation","page":"Home","text":"The paper is on arXiv (PDF). If you use this project for your research, please cite:\n\n@inproceedings{Pritts2026LocalOptimization,\n  title     = {Robust Local Optimization Done Right},\n  author    = {Pritts, James and K{\\\"o}ser, Kevin},\n  booktitle = {Asian Conference on Computer Vision (ACCV)},\n  year      = {2026},\n  eprint    = {2610.05743},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv}\n}\n\nThe classical LO baseline is RansacLib's LO-MSAC; if you use the paper project's LebedaLOPlus or LebedaIterLO arms, cite RansacLib (Sattler et al., 2019) and Lebeda, Matas and Chum (BMVC 2012) — see Baselines.","title":"Citation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Acknowledgments","page":"Home","text":"James Pritts is funded by Kiel Training for Excellence, an EU Horizon Europe programme under the Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions (MSCA), grant agreement No. 101081480, and by KMS Kiel Marine Science – Centre for Interdisciplinary Marine Science at Kiel University.","title":"Acknowledgments"}]
}
